 Halpern (2015, 8; 2016, 90–91) already distinguishes several switch models and explains the effect of normality on them. In the LT/RT route model, excluding the witness where the train fails to take the left track despite a left setting blocks the switch verdict. Under event coding that excludes a frozen default, conflicting with Theorem 1; under inertial coding it can be the license rule's penalty on an unlicensed deviant. Doorbells tests whether that latter policy transfers when both routes are occurrences. The theorem supplies a cross-case constraint within the stated class, rather than discovering that normality can struggle with switches.
 
+### Additive compensation
+
+Give a world the sum of its variables' scores. Every root gets $r>0$ at default and 0 otherwise; every non-root gets $a,b,c,d$ in categories $\mathbf n,\mathbf l,\mathbf f,\mathbf u$, respectively. The same five real numbers apply across models. Admit a witness iff its total is at least the actual total. Unlike componentwise comparison, this permits compensation. Additive representations themselves are familiar: Halpern (2016, 144–145) discusses ranking functions with addition as their combination operation. Uniform category scores are the restriction tested here.
+
+**Theorem 3.** Under event coding and the modified definition, any such score that counts $C=1$ a cause in Chain and $K=1$ a cause of survival in Boulder counts $T=1$ a cause of barking in Doorbells.
+
+*Proof.* Chain's only singleton witness world is $(C,I,Y)=(0,0,0)$, with gain $g_C=r+2a-2b$. Boulder's only singleton witness world is $(F,B,K,H)=(1,1,0,1)$, with gain $g_B=c-a$. Freezing actual values gives no other worlds changing these outcomes. In Doorbells, $[T\leftarrow0,L\leftarrow0]$ gives $(M,T,L,R,E)=(1,0,0,0,0)$, with gain
+
+$$g_T=r+a-2b+c=g_C+g_B.$$
+
+The two causal judgments require $g_C,g_B\geq0$, hence $g_T\geq0$. This valid witness makes the singleton $T=1$ minimal automatically. ∎
+
+Thus compensation alone cannot preserve those three judgments. The result does not depend on Locusts or its contested coarse outcome. It still assumes the binary route model, common scores and event coding; variable-specific scores or richer representations can escape it.
+
+The definition restriction matters. With $(r,a,b,c,d)=(2,0,-1,-5,-3)$, original and updated HP preserve Chain and Boulder while excluding the Doorbells switch. Their additional Boulder witness $(0,1,0,1)$ has gain $r+d-b=0$; the modified definition cannot change the background root $F$ to 0. The Doorbells witness has gain $-1$. Enumeration of all candidate subsets confirms that the switch is not even part of a cause under either older definition. The same score accepts genuine prevention and Locusts and excludes short-circuit prevention. The obstruction therefore arises from uniform aggregation together with the modified contingency restriction, not from additivity alone.
+
+Uniformity is stronger than invariance under renaming. Distinct mechanisms can have different statistical reliabilities or normative significance, even when their values share a category. Theorem 3 does not prohibit representing those differences. Its diagnostic is quantitative: if the root-default bonuses for Chain and Doorbells are $r_C$ and $r_T$, while category scores remain shared, the identity becomes $g_T=g_C+g_B+(r_T-r_C)$. Excluding the switch while retaining both calibration judgments requires $r_C-r_T>g_C+g_B$. Thus even this simple escape has an explicit cost: a sufficiently larger normality bonus for removing the chain's initiating event. Independent evidence might justify that difference. The theorem identifies what a uniform policy cannot supply, and what one non-uniform response must establish.
+
 ## 5. Beyond the class: an isomorph with identical defaults
 
 Halpern and Hitchcock do not commit to the class (2015, 26 fn. 13; 36 fn. 18). The obvious non-local repair looks at a mechanism's inputs: a frozen default is atypical only when some parent of the frozen variable takes a deviant value. This has a pedigree in Hitchcock's (2007, 508) Principle of Sufficient Reason, which says that a variable with parents takes a default value whenever all its parents do; a deviant value needs a deviant source. The drought equation $D=\neg Q\wedge\neg S$ violates that principle under event coding, since a deviant death follows from all-default parents, whereas the left bell's equation does not. This *trigger rule* penalizes the frozen left bell (its parent, the press, is deviant) but not the frozen maize. With the candidate's own value exempted and the train's left-track travel coded inertially, the trigger rule gives the standard verdict on every case in Table 1 under the original and updated definitions. (Under the modified definition it errs on Billy's throw and on the switch.)
